Endometriosis
Young Adult & Adolecence
Secondary dysmenorrhoea, Deep dyspareunia, Chronic pelvic pain, Pouch of Douglas nodularity

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: YAA-015
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe, unremitting pelvic pain, dyschezia, and extreme exhaustion causing inability to stand or manage basic self-care.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, Partner
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient with an acute, agonizing exacerbation of chronic pelvic pain, newly complicated by severe cyclical painful bowel movements and inability to tolerate oral intake due to pain-induced nausea. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was critically required to exclude an acute surgical complication (e.g., endometrioma rupture or bowel obstruction), and to administer potent parenteral analgesia to break the pain crisis before morning clinical availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The pain deep in my pelvis is blinding, going to the toilet is excruciating, and I am too exhausted to even sit up."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a severe acute exacerbation of a chronic non-cyclic pelvic pain that has lasted greater than six months. Currently menstruating, the patient reports severe secondary dysmenorrhoea that is entirely refractory to usual oral analgesics. The patient complains of extreme deep dyspareunia (worse premenstrually) and notable dysuria. The immediate precipitant for the urgent visit is severe dyschezia (cyclical painful bowel movements), causing the patient to actively avoid defecation and resulting in agonizing pelvic cramping. The pain is accompanied by profound fatigue and exhaustion. The patient also reports a history of recurrent pregnancy loss under investigation.
Functional Status: Bedbound; assuming a fetal position; totally reliant on partner for hydration.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient is in extreme distress, completely incapacitated by pain, and experiencing severe psychological burnout regarding the chronic nature of the condition.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 115/70 | HR: 102 | Temp: 37.0 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Patient is distressed, tearful, pale, and splinting the lower abdomen with a hot water bottle.
Physical Exam: Abdominal exam reveals generalized lower quadrant tenderness, but the abdomen is soft with no involuntary guarding, rebound tenderness, or signs of generalized peritonitis. Bowel sounds are present but sluggish. Bimanual pelvic exam (chaperoned) demonstrates a fixed retroverted uterus. There is exquisite tenderness and distinct nodularity palpated in the pouch of Douglas and along the uterosacral ligaments. No adnexal masses or fullness suggestive of a large ruptured endometrioma are appreciated.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty packets of over-the-counter NSAIDs and paracetamol are on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Patient has maximized oral Naproxen and Paracetamol/Codeine over the last 12 hours with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Pain Crisis secondary to Endometriosis (clinically suspected) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ruptured Ovarian Cyst/Endometrioma - Ruled out/unlikely due to the absence of sudden-onset, sharp, strictly unilateral peritonism, and the lack of adnexal fullness on examination, though ultrasound is required for definitive exclusion.

Acute Diverticulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to the patient's age, the lack of fever, and the clearly cyclical nature of the pain directly tied to menstruation and known dyspareunia.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to the chronicity of the symptoms (over six months), the absence of fever or purulent cervical discharge, and the classic finding of uterosacral nodularity.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (Severe pain crisis requiring parenteral intervention)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, incapacitating pain crisis driven by severe cyclical secondary dysmenorrhoea and dyschezia, set against a background of chronic pelvic pain, deep dyspareunia, and uterosacral nodularity. The clinical picture is heavily indicative of deeply infiltrating endometriosis. The failure of maximum oral analgesia and the severe functional impairment necessitated an urgent after-hours physical assessment. This evaluation safely excluded an acute surgical abdomen (such as a ruptured endometrioma or bowel obstruction), allowing for the immediate administration of parenteral analgesics and antiemetics. Delaying this intervention would have prolonged agonizing, unmanageable pain and severe distress, which could not wait for a routine morning appointment.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Ketorolac 30mg IM stat to provide potent prostaglandin inhibition bypassing the GI tract. Administered Ondansetron 4mg sublingual stat. Advised the patient that formal diagnosis requires visualization of powder-burn lesions or dense pelvic adhesions during a diagnostic laparoscopy with histological confirmation, to be arranged via a specialist.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the severe dyschezia, commence a daily osmotic laxative (e.g., Macrogol) three days before menses begins, actively keeping the stool perfectly soft and preventing the agonizing mechanical stretch of the inflamed bowel. To physically interrupt the pelvic muscle spasm caused by the pain, utilize a transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS) machine over the suprapubic area, directly scrambling the pain signals reaching the brain.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Recommended obtaining a TENS machine.
Follow-up: Urgent GP review within 7 days to initiate robust, long-term hormonal suppression (e.g., continuous progestins) and expedite a referral to an advanced laparoscopic gynaecologist.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department is required if the patient develops a high fever, begins vomiting intractable, or if the pain suddenly localizes sharply to one side and causes fainting or severe dizziness.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: A clinical diagnosis of endometriosis is highly suspected based on your exam, but definitive proof requires a surgical laparoscopy.
Clinical Course: The injected medication will break the acute pain peak tonight, but chronic, multi-disciplinary management involving hormones and potentially surgery will be required for long-term control.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Go to the nearest Emergency Department immediately if you develop a fever, if your abdomen becomes rigid and board-like, or if you feel faint and dizzy when trying to stand up.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Endometriosis means tissue similar to your uterine lining is growing outside the uterus, causing extreme inflammation, scarring, and pain on your surrounding organs, like your bowel. The injection tonight blocks the severe inflammatory chemicals causing the acute crisis.
Actionable Steps: Start taking a gentle stool softener a few days before your period starts to prevent the excruciating pain when going to the toilet. Discuss continuous hormonal therapies with your GP next week to stop your periods and suppress the disease.

